4,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Nick Rallis"},{"season":2024,"team":"CAR","distance":0.3972,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Ejiro Evero"},{"season":2023,"team":"NE","distance":0.4259,"shared":4,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Ravens are 4-4 through week 8 of the 2026 season and have been outscored 203 to 179. The offense ranks 14th in EPA per play and the defense 18th in EPA allowed, so neither side has stood out much yet.","offense":"The Ravens offense has been a little above average by EPA per play so far. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Tyler Huntley has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Zay Flowers, who has drawn 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Ravens defense has been about average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been explosive play rate allowed, where it ranks 10th, and its weakest has been EPA allowed per rush, where it ranks 23rd. Tavius Robinson leads the team with 3.5 sacks, and Mike Green has added 3.5 despite playing only 49% of the snaps. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 5th lowest in the league.","special":"The Ravens have been one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 17% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers have made 76% of field goals, which is well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 21% of the money on the books. Their defensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job has been split, with Tyler Huntley and Lamar Jackson both getting meaningful snaps. Zay Flowers has been the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 608 receiving yards. Derrick Henry has handled most of the work in the backfield with 83% of the carries. On defense, Tavius Robinson leads the team with 3.5 sacks.","coaching":"Declan Doyle is calling the offense and Jesse Minter is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Titans and the 2025 Bills under Joe Brady. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Jaguars and the 2023 Chargers under Brandon Staley.","trends":"The offense went from 12th in EPA per play in 2025 to 14th so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 20th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 18th so far this season, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was number of pass rushers, which went from 4.23 to 4.46.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}